A full-marks implementation should read the filename from standard input, read the two strings from that file, compute the dynamic programming recurrence for longest anchored common subsequence, and print only the integer length.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Anchored {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        BufferedReader terminal = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        String filename = terminal.readLine();

        BufferedReader file = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(filename));
        String s1 = file.readLine();
        String s2 = file.readLine();
        file.close();

        int n = s1.length();
        int m = s2.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][m + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {
                char a = s1.charAt(i - 1);
                char b = s2.charAt(j - 1);

                if (a == b) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else if (a == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];
                } else if (b == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println(dp[n][m]);
    }
}
```

This implementation runs in $O(nm)$ time and uses $O(nm)$ memory. Since each input string has length at most $2000$, the table has at most about four million entries, which is suitable for the stated limit.
